WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.390
This video is sponsored by Squarespace.

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:08.360
When American astronaut Scott Kelly spent
a year at the International Space Station

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:15.961
in 2015 and 2016, his heart shrank by 25%
in mass according to a study published this year.

00:00:15.961 --> 00:00:19.279
Without the pull of gravity, the heart doesn’t
have to pump as hard.

00:00:19.279 --> 00:00:23.039
Studies like these are being done as space
opens up to more people who will journey

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:25.599
deeper into the solar system.

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:27.440
Space travel is hard on the body.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.619
For every month of flight, you lose about
1.5% of bone density according to NASA.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.829
Bones don’t have to support the body against
gravity.

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:38.460
The farther you are from Earth, the weaker
the gravitational force.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.950
For longer trips - say, going to Mars - which
takes about six months each way - that would

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.780
mean losing roughly 10% of your total bone
density.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.449
Your weaker bones would be more prone to fractures.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:53.550
After spending half a year in space, recovery
could take 3 to 4 years.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:58.010
The best way to build up bone mass is to do
weight-bearing exercises - in which your bones

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.420
support your weight.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.890
Like tennis, skip rope, or jogging.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:06.462
Running on a treadmill at the ISS is a little
different as astronauts have to strap themselves in.

00:01:06.462 --> 00:01:10.830
Despite the effort, you’ll likely only be
able to slow down the rate of bone loss - not

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.660
prevent it altogether.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.220
Artificial gravity could help.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.720
A proposed private space hotel called Voyager
is designed to spin slowly to mimic the effects

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:21.580
of a gravitational force.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.740
Besides losing bone density, muscles can weaken
from lack of use and deteriorate.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:32.060
Research from previous missions shows astronauts
lose up to 20% of muscle mass on flights lasting

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.990
less than two weeks.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:38.180
Many astronauts also report back pain, as
weakened muscles provide less support for

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.180
the spine.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.320
In order to combat muscle loss, a machine
called an ARED imitates weight lifting to

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.960
work all the major muscle groups and decrease
the recovery time later.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.480
Though again, it doesn’t get better right
away once astronauts return to Earth.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.750
They’re at a much higher risk of a herniated
disc.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.730
The lack of gravity is also problematic for
the cardiovascular system since there’s

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:00.090
nothing to pull blood to the lower part of
the body.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:05.210
Instead, it goes to the chest and head, causing
astronauts to have puffy faces and bulging

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:07.320
blood vessels in their necks.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.841
Canadian researchers have discovered that
this chronic increase in blood pressure to

00:02:10.841 --> 00:02:15.540
the head and neck can result in stiffer arteries
in which a six-month mission is similar to

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.930
ageing 10 to 20 years on Earth.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:24.459
NASA requires aspiring astronauts to have
a blood pressure reading of no more than 140/90

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:25.769
measured in a sitting position.

00:02:25.769 --> 00:02:30.260
They’ll also have to pass physical tests
in a swimming pool - the best place to simulate

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:33.019
the feeling of weightlessness in space.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:38.780
Candidates have to swim three laps of a 25-meter
pool non-stop which doesn’t sound that difficult.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.250
But then they have to repeat it.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:45.140
This time wearing a blue flight suit and
tennis shoes and then tread water for 10 minutes.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.530
However, being physically fit won’t protect
against the very serious threat of radiation.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.640
We’re protected from some of the most dangerous
rays and particles because of Earth’s magnetic field.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:01.546
But beyond low Earth orbit is a world teeming
with radiation which is measured using Milli-Sievert (mSv).

00:03:01.546 --> 00:03:05.349
1 mSv is the equivalent of about three chest
x-rays.

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:11.849
A six-month trip to the ISS or the moon exposes
astronauts to doses ranging from 50 to 2,000

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:16.239
mSvs - that would be like having 150 to 6,000
chest x-rays.

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:21.730
And it’s ionizing radiation - it can destroy
cells and damage DNA, increasing the risk

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:24.520
of cancer and degenerative diseases.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:28.980
Protecting against radiation on the moon or
Mars means building habitats with reliable shielding.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:32.579
But in transit, you’re only protected by
what’s around you.

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:37.209
A strategy tested by NASA’s space radiation
specialists involves crew members barricading

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:39.180
themselves with stowage bags.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:44.040
Other techniques being developed include wearable
vests that add another layer of mass and electrically

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.670
charged surfaces that can deflect these dangerous
particles.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:51.680
Exposure to radiation experienced by Scott
Kelly is believed to have activated certain

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.159
genes known to encode proteins that help fix
damaged DNA.

00:03:56.159 --> 00:04:00.190
Researchers gleaned all of this information
by comparing Scott to his identical twin Mark,

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:03.700
also an astronaut, who remained on Earth as
the control subject.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.780
The stresses of being in orbit appear to have
altered Scott’s immune system, worsened

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:14.400
his eyesight, and, oddly, lengthened the caps
at the ends of his DNA strands called telomeres.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.190
These protect chromosomes - kind of like how
plastic handles protect jump ropes.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:20.730
Usually, they shorten as we age.

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.710
The strange lengthening disappeared a few
days after he returned to Earth.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:30.760
However, seven per cent of his gene expression still remained
altered six months later.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.850
More research is now being done to study the
lasting impact of space travel on our bodies.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:39.791
Not to mention there are behavioral effects
of being restricted to a spacecraft for a

00:04:39.791 --> 00:04:41.360
long period of time.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.527
To make astronauts feel a little less isolated,
they got internet access on the ISS a decade ago.

00:04:46.527 --> 00:04:51.570
Being able to go online and access websites
is a crucial way of communicating these days,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:54.760
